<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chulalongkorn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1853">1853</a>–<a href="/years/1910">1910</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Rama V</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chulalongkorn, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> V of Siam, reigned from 1868 to 1910. He abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and forced labour in stages, created ministries, a centralised provincial administration, railways and modern schools, and sent princes to study in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. To preserve Siam's independence he ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and four Malay states to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. He is revered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> as the Great Beloved King.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn.jpg" alt="Chulalongkorn" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bain News Service, publisher. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chulalongkorn_LoC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Chulalongkorn</b> began the gradual abolition of slavery in 1874, a process completed by the Slave Abolition Act of 1905.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1896 King <b>Chulalongkorn</b> of Siam was allowed to take home eight cartloads of sculptures from Borobudur; several of them, among them lions and a guardian statue, are now in the National Museum in Bangkok.</blockquote><a href="/work/borobudur">Borobudur · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In Siam two former British officers adopted God Save the King as the king's honour music in 1851; when King <b>Chulalongkorn</b> visited Singapore in 1871 and found Siam using the same anthem as Great Britain, he had a tune of its own chosen.</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li><li><blockquote>It was abolished in stages from 1874 to 1905, under King <b>Chulalongkorn</b>, and legally ended only in 1915.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ayutthaya">Social Hierarchy of the Ayutthaya Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1871 King <b>Chulalongkorn</b> visited Singapore and Batavia (now Jakarta) and found that Siam was using the same anthem as Great Britain, which then ruled Singapore.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sansoen-phra-barami">Sansoen Phra Barami</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.75,100.52&amp;year=1890" aria-label="Bangkok on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7304 1652 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Chulalongkorn"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7754 1922h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7754 1922h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.75,100.52&amp;year=1890">What was here in 1890? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
